import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"American history begins long before 1492, with millions of Native people living in diverse societies.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Native American societies adapted to their environments: farming villages in the Southwest and East, buffalo hunters on the Plains, and fishing peoples in the Pacific Northwest. They had complex governments, trade networks and religions."},{"t":"key","text":"After 1492, the Columbian Exchange moved plants, animals and diseases between the Americas and Europe. Diseases like smallpox killed huge numbers of Native people."},{"t":"p","text":"Spain conquered the Aztec and Inca empires and built a large colonial empire. France traded furs along the St. Lawrence and Mississippi rivers, and England founded colonies on the Atlantic coast."}]},{"say":"England's thirteen colonies grew into three distinct regions with different economies and ways of life.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["New England: founded by Puritans seeking religious freedom; small farms, fishing, shipbuilding and town meetings.","Middle colonies: diverse and religiously tolerant (Pennsylvania was founded by Quakers); fertile farms grew grain.","Southern colonies: plantations growing tobacco, rice and indigo, increasingly worked by enslaved Africans."]},{"t":"key","text":"The colonies developed traditions of self-government, like the Virginia House of Burgesses (1619) and the Mayflower Compact (1620)."},{"t":"p","text":"Slavery grew throughout the colonies, especially in the South, where enslaved Africans were forced to work on plantations. The Atlantic slave trade brought millions of Africans to the Americas."}]}],[{"say":"After 1763, Britain tightened control over its colonies, and colonists pushed back.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"After the French and Indian War, Britain was in debt and taxed the colonies with the Stamp Act (1765), the Townshend Acts and the Tea Act. Colonists protested \"no taxation without representation.\""},{"t":"key","text":"Colonists protested with boycotts, the Sons of Liberty, the Boston Tea Party (1773) and the First Continental Congress. Britain responded with the Intolerable Acts."},{"t":"p","text":"Fighting began at Lexington and Concord in 1775. Thomas Paine's Common Sense convinced many to support independence, and on July 4, 1776, Congress adopted the Declaration of Independence, written mainly by Thomas Jefferson."}]},{"say":"Americans won independence, then designed a government that has lasted over two centuries.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"With help from France after the victory at Saratoga, George Washington's army won at Yorktown (1781). The Treaty of Paris (1783) recognized American independence."},{"t":"key","text":"The weak Articles of Confederation were replaced by the Constitution (1787), which created three branches of government with checks and balances."},{"t":"p","text":"Key compromises: the Great Compromise created a House based on population and a Senate with equal representation; the Three-Fifths Compromise counted enslaved people as three-fifths for representation. The Bill of Rights (1791) protected freedoms like speech, religion and trial by jury."}]}],[{"say":"In the early 1800s, the United States grew in size, population and economic power.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Louisiana Purchase (1803) doubled the nation's size. The War of 1812 boosted national pride. Later, the idea of Manifest Destiny drove expansion to the Pacific, including Texas, Oregon and land won in the Mexican-American War."},{"t":"key","text":"Westward expansion came at a terrible cost to Native Americans. The Indian Removal Act (1830) forced tribes like the Cherokee west on the Trail of Tears."},{"t":"p","text":"New inventions like the cotton gin, steamboats, canals and railroads transformed the economy. Reform movements fought to end slavery (abolition), expand women's rights (the Seneca Falls Convention, 1848) and improve education."}]},{"say":"Slavery divided the nation more and more until compromise was no longer possible.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The North industrialized while the South depended on cotton grown by enslaved people. As new territories formed, the question of whether they would allow slavery became explosive."},{"t":"key","text":"Compromises (the Missouri Compromise, 1820, and the Compromise of 1850) tried to balance free and slave states but failed to settle the issue."},{"t":"p","text":"The Fugitive Slave Act angered Northerners. The Kansas-Nebraska Act led to violence in \"Bleeding Kansas.\" The Dred Scott decision (1857) ruled that enslaved people were not citizens. When Abraham Lincoln was elected in 1860, Southern states seceded."}]}],[{"say":"The Civil War was America's deadliest conflict, fought to preserve the Union and, eventually, to end slavery.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The war began at Fort Sumter in 1861. The North had more people, factories and railroads; the South had strong military leaders and fought on familiar ground."},{"t":"key","text":"Lincoln's Emancipation Proclamation (1863) declared freedom for enslaved people in Confederate states and allowed African Americans to join the Union army."},{"t":"p","text":"Gettysburg (1863) was a turning point, and Lincoln's Gettysburg Address honored the dead and defined the war as a struggle for freedom and equality. General Lee surrendered at Appomattox in 1865. Lincoln was assassinated days later. The 13th Amendment abolished slavery."}]},{"say":"After the war, the nation tried to rebuild the South and protect the freedom of four million formerly enslaved people.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The 14th Amendment (1868) made all people born in the US citizens with equal protection of the laws. The 15th Amendment (1870) protected the right to vote regardless of race."},{"t":"p","text":"The Freedmen's Bureau built schools and helped freedpeople. African Americans voted and held office for the first time."},{"t":"p","text":"But many white Southerners resisted. Black Codes and later Jim Crow laws enforced segregation; groups like the Ku Klux Klan used terror. Sharecropping kept many families in poverty. Reconstruction ended in 1877 when federal troops left the South."}]}],[{"say":"After the Civil War, the US became the world's leading industrial power, transformed by factories, railroads and immigrants.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Railroads connected the country. Industries like steel (Andrew Carnegie) and oil (John D. Rockefeller) grew into huge corporations. Inventions like electric lights and the telephone changed daily life."},{"t":"key","text":"Millions of immigrants from Europe and Asia came for jobs and freedom. They worked in factories and lived in crowded city tenements, often facing discrimination."},{"t":"p","text":"Workers faced long hours, low pay and dangerous conditions, so they formed labor unions and went on strike. In the West, farmers and settlers moved onto Native lands, and Native nations were forced onto reservations."}]},{"say":"Around 1900, reformers called Progressives worked to fix the problems of industrial America, while the US also expanded overseas.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Muckrakers (journalists like Upton Sinclair) exposed corruption and unsafe conditions. Progressives won reforms: food safety laws, breaking up monopolies, child labor limits and conservation of natural lands under Theodore Roosevelt."},{"t":"key","text":"Progressive amendments brought the income tax (16th), direct election of senators (17th), Prohibition (18th) and women's right to vote (19th, 1920)."},{"t":"p","text":"The US also became an imperial power, gaining Hawaii, and after the Spanish-American War (1898), Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines. It built the Panama Canal."}]}],[{"say":"America joined the Great War, then roared into a decade of prosperity and change.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The US entered World War I in 1917 after German submarine attacks on American ships and the Zimmermann Telegram. American troops helped the Allies win in 1918. President Wilson proposed the League of Nations, but the US never joined."},{"t":"key","text":"The 1920s brought cars, radio, movies and jazz. The Harlem Renaissance celebrated African American culture. Women, newly able to vote, gained more freedom."},{"t":"p","text":"It was also a time of tension: Prohibition fueled organized crime, the Ku Klux Klan revived, and new laws limited immigration."}]},{"say":"The economy collapsed in 1929, the government stepped in, and then America fought a second world war.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The stock market crash of 1929 began the Great Depression. Banks failed and about a quarter of workers lost their jobs. The Dust Bowl devastated farms on the Plains."},{"t":"key","text":"President Franklin D. Roosevelt's New Deal created jobs programs, Social Security and bank protections, expanding the role of the federal government."},{"t":"p","text":"After Japan attacked Pearl Harbor on December 7, 1941, the US entered World War II. Factories built weapons, and women took industrial jobs. Japanese Americans were unjustly sent to internment camps. The Allies won in 1945 after D-Day, and the war ended after atomic bombs were dropped on Japan."}]}],[{"say":"After World War II, America faced a long rivalry with the Soviet Union, and a movement for equality at home.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Cold War was a struggle between the democratic, capitalist US and the communist Soviet Union. The US tried to contain communism through alliances (NATO), aid (the Marshall Plan), and wars in Korea and Vietnam. Both sides built nuclear weapons, and the Cuban Missile Crisis (1962) nearly led to nuclear war."},{"t":"key","text":"The civil rights movement used nonviolent protest to end segregation. Brown v. Board of Education (1954) ended school segregation, and the Civil Rights Act (1964) and Voting Rights Act (1965) outlawed discrimination."},{"t":"p","text":"Leaders like Martin Luther King Jr. and Rosa Parks, and thousands of ordinary people, marched, boycotted and held sit-ins."}]},{"say":"Since the 1970s, America has faced political scandals, economic changes, new technology and new challenges.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Watergate scandal led President Nixon to resign in 1974. The 1980s brought President Reagan's conservative policies, and the Cold War ended when the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991."},{"t":"key","text":"The September 11, 2001 terrorist attacks led to wars in Afghanistan and Iraq and new security measures at home."},{"t":"p","text":"The internet and globalization transformed the economy. The 2008 financial crisis caused a deep recession. In 2008, Barack Obama became the first African American president. Debates continue over immigration, health care, climate change and civil rights."}]}]];
export default text;
